index 000000000000..6811e0bf2085
--- /dev/null
+++ b/llvm/docs/AMDGPUAsmStrictPolicies.rst
@@ -0,0 +1,81 @@
+===========================================
+AMDGPU strict register and private policies
+===========================================
+
+The experimental string function attributes ``"amdgpu-no-gpr-spill"`` and
+``"amdgpu-no-private-memory"`` request fail-closed validation of the generated
+machine code. They are intended for Mono kernels calling external assembly
+through the versioned AMDGPU ASM call contract. The attributes are opt-in and
+do not change ordinary AMDGPU code generation when absent.
+
+Semantics
+=========
+
+``amdgpu-no-gpr-spill`` rejects lowered SGPR/VGPR spill operations, SGPR
+callee-saved spills into VGPR lanes, callee-saved memory saves, and whole-wave
+register spills. Copies to unused SGPRs remain permitted. The test uses the
+backend's spill provenance and frame-lowering records, not an instruction-name
+search: an algorithm's ordinary lane operation is not intrinsically a spill.
+
+``amdgpu-no-private-memory`` rejects private frames, incoming stack arguments,
+dynamic stack or dynamic-VGPR scratch reservations, and instructions that may
+access private memory. It includes default-ABI save frames, not only spill
+statistics. A private-address-space load is rejected even if the function has
+no stack frame. An unresolved generic address that may address private memory
+is conservatively rejected.
+
+Both policies propagate from an annotated function to its transitive direct
+callees in the compilation module. Unknown external callees, indirect calls
+and recursion are rejected. External declarations must have a supported ASM
+call contract with zero private memory. Their machine code is not present in
+the IR module: a separate object auditor and identity check are still required
+before linking or publishing the executable. These attributes do not prove a
+supplier's declaration truthful.
+
+Nonempty inline assembly and module-level assembly are rejected under either
+policy because the generated instruction stream is opaque to this validator.
+An empty inline asm with register constraints/clobbers is permitted. This is an
+intentional conservative limitation; it must not be worked around by asserting
+that all unknown assembly has zero private memory.
+
+Checks run at the assembly-printer boundary, after allocation, frame lowering,
+and register scavenging. The current implementation tries normal allocation
+and rejects disallowed results; it does not provide a different register
+allocator or promise to find every possible spill-free assignment. A strict
+success guarantees the checked properties subject to external contracts,
+while a failure may be an allocation limitation rather than a mathematical
+impossibility. Lowered spill counters can conservatively record spills that a
+later pass removed. Such cases may be rejected rather than silently accepted.
+
+These attributes do not permit the compiler to introduce hidden LDS/global
+context saves. No such fallback is added by this implementation. Normal
+algorithmic memory accesses remain legal when their address space excludes
+private memory.
+
+Machine-readable report
+=======================
+
+Use ``-amdgpu-asm-contract-report=/path/to/report.json`` with ``llc``. The
+parent directory must exist. The report is emitted for either assembly or
+object output, with schema ``amdgpu.asm_contract_report.v1``. The top-level
+``strict_policy_status`` is ``checked``, ``rejected`` or ``not_requested``;
+``external_object_audit_required`` explicitly remains true.
+
+Each compiled function records inherited policies, lowered spill dword counts,
+SGPR ABI lane saves, ABI memory saves, whole-wave spill register counts,
+private frame and incoming stack-argument bytes, dynamic stack status,
+potential private accesses, and analyzed numbered GPR extents.
+
+Each machine call records its target, fixed-register contract attributes,
+allocatable GPRs preserved by the actual call instruction, and physical GPRs
+live through that instruction. The latter is computed by backward physical
+liveness and excludes call definitions and regmask clobbers. It describes the
+point *after argument preparation*, not the entire argument-copy/call/result
+sequence. It does not claim to reconstruct source-level variable identities.
+The full-wave precondition is labeled ``caller_asserted``; the current backend
+does not prove arbitrary CTA/wave participation.
+
+A report with ``checked`` is a compiler-side policy result, not proof that a
+subsequently linked external object matches the declared contract. A driver
+must also check successful compilation, the final linked executable, object
+hashes, and any execution-state preconditions before treating it as verified.
